is it like two pointers always work with sorted arrays
Mostly yes, but not always. Here's the full picture:

Why sorting helps two pointers
Two pointers work by making a directional decision — "move left" or "move right." That only works when you can predict what moving in a direction will do to your result.

Sorted arrays give you that guarantee:

Moving right → value increases. Moving left → value decreases.

Without sorting, moving right could give you anything — you'd have no basis for a decision.

Cases where sorting is NOT needed
| Problem | Why no sort needed |
|---|---|| Linked List cycle detection (fast/slow pointers) | Pointers move at different speeds, not based on values |
| Remove duplicates from sorted array | Already sorted by definition |
| Sliding window (subarray sum, longest substring) | Pointers define a window, not comparing values |
| Palindrome check | Just comparing characters from both ends |
| Merge two sorted arrays | Already sorted |

The real rule
Two pointers need predictability — some property that tells you which direction to move. Sorting is the most common way to get that, but not the only way.

The pattern is really:


Can I decide "move left or right" based on current state?
├── Yes, via sorted order     → sort first
├── Yes, via window size      → sliding window (no sort)
└── Yes, via pointer speed    → fast/slow pointers (no sort)
For LeetCode problems specifically
If you see:

"find pairs/triplets/quadruplets that sum to X" → sort + two pointers
"subarray with condition" → sliding window (no sort)
"cycle in linked list" → fast/slow (no sort)
Sorting + two pointers is one flavor of the two-pointer family — just the most common one you'll see in array problems.